    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "When children express themselves freely through making, they nurture critical character traits such as confidence and creative thinking. However, in a time when families are staying at home, parents often struggle to find high-quality hands-on learning experiences for their children, especially with limited time and materials. As a consequence, children spend more time passively watching screens and less time engaging with their environment and learning from these interactions.\r\n \r\nDowit provides a series of online workshops that encourage young children to tinker, design, and build with recycled materials found at home. It provides a safe space for their self-expression. Additionally, Dowit's mobile app encourages children to document their creations, reflect on their process, and celebrate their learning. I conducted a total of 53 workshops for over 330 children from four to eight years old. Children who participated regularly in the workshops showed an increase in their creative confidence and started independently creating different types of artifacts using materials found at home. Their parents reported that their children displayed creativity in ways they hadn’t seen before and that they started to form habits such as saving cardboard and paper rolls for the purpose of making.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
